<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ballot Act 1872</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ballot Act 1872 was an act of the UK Parliament that introduced the secret ballot for parliamentary and local elections. Passed by Gladstone's first government, it replaced open public voting, which had exposed voters to bribery and to intimidation by landlords and employers, and was first used at the Pontefract by-election of 1872.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Before the act, nomination and election in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took place at the hustings, and votes were cast in the open. Employers and landowners could sway their employees and tenants by being present, or by sending agents to watch the votes being cast, and small retailers feared upsetting their bigger customers. Radicals such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartists</a> had long campaigned for a secret ballot. The Representation of the People Act 1867, the Second Reform Act, gave the vote to skilled working men in the boroughs, and many felt that such voters would be especially open to bribery, intimidation or blackmail.</p><p>John Bright warned that tenants who voted against their landlord's wishes could be threatened with eviction, and his brother-in-law Edward Aldam Leatham introduced the bill. Many in the Establishment held that a patron's pressure on his tenants was legitimate and that secret voting was unmanly and cowardly. Lord Russell opposed a culture of secrecy in elections, which he saw as an obvious prelude to universal suffrage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The act, 35 &amp; 36 Vict. c. 33, required parliamentary and local government elections in the United Kingdom to be held by secret ballot and abolished the traditional hustings. Ballot papers were numbered, and the officer issuing a paper wrote the voter's electoral roll number on its matching counterfoil, so that in principle a vote could still be traced to the voter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The first secret ballot was held on 15 August 1872, when Hugh Childers was re-elected at Pontefract after being made Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster. The ballot box, sealed in wax with a liquorice stamp, is kept at Pontefract museum; 16% of those who voted were illiterate, and special arrangements had to be made for them. The general election of 1874, the first by secret ballot, returned the first Conservative majority since 1841.</p><p>The act mattered most in Ireland, where tenants could now vote against the landlord class, and its main result was seen in the general election of 1880, which ended the landlord interest in both Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. The secret ballot may have done something to reduce bribery, but many see the Corrupt and Illegal Practices Prevention Act 1883 as the key law against electoral corruption. The act inspired the Belgian minister Jules Malou's law of 9 July 1877 on the secrecy of the vote, and it was repealed by the Representation of the People Act 1949.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ballot Act 1872” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…to much of the middle class; further Reform Acts in 1867 and 1884 enfranchised most male householders, the <b>Ballot Act</b> of 1872 introduced secret voting, and the Representation of the People Act of 1918 gave the vote to almost all men over 21 and to most women over…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
